/*
 * libz -- the jump table, in order.
 *
 * ===================================================================
 *  THE ORDER OF THIS FILE IS AN ABI. NEVER INSERT IN THE MIDDLE.
 * ===================================================================
 *
 * Every zcc-compiled binary calls into libz by INDEX, not by name:
 * `jal ra, table + 4*i`. So inserting an entry anywhere but the end
 * shifts every later index, and a binary built against the old order
 * silently calls the wrong function. Not a crash -- printf where
 * malloc was expected -- which is the worst kind of wrong.
 *
 * This is the same discipline sw/common/syscalls.def documents for the
 * same reason, and the failure mode there is on record: HID_READ_KEY
 * was originally inserted ahead of MSG_SEND and had to be moved.
 *
 * Two protections exist and neither replaces reading this comment:
 *
 *  - LIBZ_ABI_VERSION (libz.h) is bumped whenever the table changes,
 *    stamped into the blob, and checked by zcc against the value in
 *    libz.syms. A stale blob is refused rather than mis-called.
 *  - The build refuses to produce a table SHORTER than the previous
 *    one, since that can only mean an entry was removed.
 *
 * Adding an entry at the end is always safe: an old binary simply
 * never reaches it.
 */

